  "preamble": "The shift from the voiced labio-velar approximant [w] to the voiced labiodental fricative [v] is a widespread fortition process in the history of Indo-European languages. It involves the loss of the velar component and the strengthening of the labial constriction from an approximant to a fricative. In the Jê family, Apinajé (Northern Jê) and Laklãnõ (Southern Jê) independently changed *w to v. Classical Armenian ւ /w/ after vowels is [v] in both modern standards (հաւ 'hen' > հավ [hɑv]).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fortition, De-velarization",
